    "path": "issues/cli.md"
  },
  {
    "@id": "ArtifactNode:00CF1WZ3S0000",
    "@type": "ArtifactNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "ArtifactNode:00CF1WZ3S0000:props:StringProp:00CN3Z21F0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "description",
        "value": "The engine-level bug family this concern owns — most prominently a still-unexplained per-operation slowdown that compounds the longer a long-lived process stays alive, once bad enough to hang the whole service for 40 minutes."
      }
    ],
    "title": "core.md",
    "text": "**`npm run verify` costs ~5 minutes, dominated by a process-lifetime slowdown neither store-freshness nor heap size explains** — instrumented per-step (a `console.log`-wrapping timer keyed on each step's own `\"N. Testing...\"` header, printing a sorted breakdown even on failure) and found the same operation (`checkLinkIntegrity`'s corpus sweep, and separately `parseMarkdownTree`-driven ingestion in Linking Slice steps 13-18) costs low hundreds of ms freshly isolated, but 12-55 *seconds* run as `verify.ts`'s own later steps — steps 3-6 doing the identical kind of parsing work stay fast, steps 7-12 in between do no heavy parsing at all, then 13 onward is uniformly slow. Two credible causes ruled out by direct experiment, not by inspection: (1) oxigraph's own WASM memory footprint (`external`, ~850MB-1GB by that point vs ~50MB fresh) looked causal, but compacting the store mid-run (dehydrate a scratch dir, rehydrate a brand-new `Store` with the identical live content) made the same sweep *slower*, not faster — ruling out \"this one store instance is bloated.\" (2) V8 heap-size tuning (`--max-old-space-size=8192 --max-semi-space-size=128`) changed nothing measurably.",
    "parent": "FolderNode:00CDBYV4BR003",
    "type": "root",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG001"
    ],
